00:05:40.495 --> 00:05:41.870
TAICHI NAKASHIMA:
All the Mercari

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.260
is running on the on-premise
in Japanese provider.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.630
So we are trying to migrate from
on-premise to the GCP platform

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:50.230
now.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:50.750
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.208
And you specifically-- I think
you said as well-- you said,

00:05:53.208 --> 00:05:56.166
specifically, you're working
on GKE or Kubernetes engine.

00:05:56.166 --> 00:05:57.290
Is the platform doing that?

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.850
Can you tell us a bit more
about how that's going?

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.550
TERRY LI: His team
actually started

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.050
working on that platform that
utilizes GKE and other GCP

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.770
products to allow us to develop
services on that platform.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.150
So on my team, we're trying
to deploy new microservices

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:15.900
into that platform.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.919
So far, it's been
going well, I'd say.

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:19.710
We have a couple of
services running on it.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.620
It allows the
developers to iterate

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.640
on their services in
a much faster speed

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:30.272
without relying on a SRE team
to deploy their code for them.

00:06:30.272 --> 00:06:31.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.590
What made you decide to use GKE?

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.290
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: So we
want deploy the application

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.050
as a container, because
in microservice world,

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:45.540
each sub-stream should have the
freedom to choose a software

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:46.920
programming language.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.330
So infrastructure point of
view, it reads everything

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.590
as part of a container.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.890
It's easy to handle,
because we don't

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.520
need take care about
the application

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.470
is reading Ruby,
or Python, or Go.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.600
So we want to use a container.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.870
So for deploying
the container, I

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:08.580
think the kind that is a
best choice is Kubernetes.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.030
And the best management
service is GKE,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.527
so that's why we chose GKE.

00:07:12.527 --> 00:07:14.360
MELANIE WARRICK: Did
you have any challenges

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:16.700
with implementing GKE?

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.735
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: Challenges--
on GKE or Kubernetes,

00:07:19.735 --> 00:07:21.240
we can do anything.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.170
So for example, if we
don't use namespace--

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.440
so every thing got
default namespace,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.994
and it's getting complex.

00:07:29.994 --> 00:07:31.160
Everything is one namespace.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.230
So we need set up the
[INAUDIBLE] namespace

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.090
for each microservices.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.630
Also, we need provides
the proper authorization

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.490
to each micro sub-stream.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.820
So we need set up some RBAC.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.560
And this stuff is
challenging point for us.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.310
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:47.900
Do you automate any of that?

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.780
Do you do a continuous
integration?

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:51.740
You're nodding at me.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:52.760
So I'm going to go
with you're currently

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:53.970
doing that kind of stuff.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:57.080
TERRY LI: Yeah, we use a lot
of tools to automate that.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.640
For example, we
never actually go

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.550
to GCP console to actually
activate by clicking a button.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.510
We use Terraform to
define what we need.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.610
We use Spinnaker to actually
deploy our container

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.800
to GKE, things like that.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.008
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, great.

00:08:12.008 --> 00:08:13.520
So what are some
upcoming products

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.520
that you're excited about in
regards to the current platform

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.450
that you've built or
your current setup?

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.750
TERRY LI: I'm excited
about GKE on-prem

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.630
that you guys just announced,
because we actually

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:28.080
had some challenges interacting
between our on-prem data center

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.350
with the services in GKE.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.730
And having to deploy
some service in GKE

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:36.140
and maintain some legacy systems
in the on-prem data center

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.070
is kind of a burden.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.770
So if we have GKE
on-prem, we could

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:45.500
deploy all that microservices
both in GKE and the data

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.130
centers, so that developers can
focus on their business logic,

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.097
and the deploy can be
done to both sides.

00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:52.430
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.450
I'd love to go back
to what you were

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.010
talking about before as well.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.750
You were talking
about using Spinnaker

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:57.800
and that kind of stuff.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:59.425
I think, for people
who are potentially

00:08:59.425 --> 00:09:01.520
thinking about
CI/CD pipelines, how

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.680
have you found that
experience using Spinnaker?

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.412
[INAUDIBLE] use something
for testing as well.

00:09:06.412 --> 00:09:08.620
How does that normally look
inside what you've built?

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.130
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: The reason
to choose the Spinnaker?

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:10.560
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:11.976
TAICHI NAKASHIMA:
In microservice,

00:09:11.976 --> 00:09:14.270
each back-end
service engineer need

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:17.180
to handle deploy themselves.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.550
So in [INAUDIBLE],, maybe
central operation team

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:21.840
is doing this stuff.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.785
But microservices, each
team needs to do this.

00:09:24.785 --> 00:09:28.960
So we need some way to easily
set up a deployment pipeline.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:33.110
In kubectl, if you
[INAUDIBLE],, we can deploy.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:36.610
But if just kubectl
it does not make sure

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.100
that deployment
is worked or not.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.080
So because of some
results quota shortage,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.790
maybe deployment may fail.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:47.980
But if only kubectl
it's difficult to find

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:51.530
the deployment [INAUDIBLE]
we need to check by kubectl

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.220
describe what it disk want.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.310
So that's why we need some
special tool for deployment.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.230
And we found that Spinnaker is
easy to create the deployment

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:00.950
pipeline.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:05.880
And it also checks kubectl
Opera is worked or not.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.310
And if the [INAUDIBLE] fail,
it [INAUDIBLE] pipelines.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.414
So that's why we chose
Spinnaker for deployment.

00:10:11.414 --> 00:10:12.830
MARK MANDEL: So
do your developers

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:15.327
have a specific place
they have to put code?

00:10:15.327 --> 00:10:16.910
If I'm a developer
working on Mercari,

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:19.810
and I'm like, I wrote some code,
how does Spinnaker find it,

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.730
and how does that flow look?

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:25.000
TERRY LI: We use GitHub
to manage our code.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.280
So every code change
goes to GitHub.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.460
It will open you
in a pull request.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.070
They can configure their cr
tool to build their image

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.930
for that PR.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.290
And their image will
be posted to GCR.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:41.960
Spinnaker has the ability
to listen to a repository

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:48.060
and find the images defined
by some regular expression.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.080
So it can automatically
find the image that's

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:52.730
updated for this application.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:56.270
And you can choose to
automatically deploy

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.982
that image to GKE.

00:10:57.982 --> 00:10:59.940
MELANIE WARRICK: In the
pipeline that you have,

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.856
is there anything you
wish someone would build

00:11:01.856 --> 00:11:03.390
that would make it easier?

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.015
TERRY LI: There's
one interesting story

00:11:05.015 --> 00:11:08.170
about having that
ability to just make

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.340
Spinnaker listen to some
repository and deploy stuff.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.220
It used to be not working right.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.410
And then starting from one day--

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:16.850
I think, a few months ago--

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:18.660
it suddenly started working.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.270
So it automatically built
all images in that repository

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:24.030
to the cluster.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:27.380
So it's trying to deploy
all the historical versions

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.169
of our services
that were terrible.

00:11:30.169 --> 00:11:31.460
MARK MANDEL: But it's fine now.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:32.480
You sorted it out now.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:33.806
TERRY LI: It's fine now.

00:11:33.806 --> 00:11:35.174
Yeah.

00:11:35.174 --> 00:11:36.500
MELANIE WARRICK: That's good.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.416
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: And
[INAUDIBLE],, everything

00:11:38.416 --> 00:11:40.760
need to be configured by GUI.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:44.480
So we want manage
the pipeline in code

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.755
so that it's easy to manage.

00:11:45.755 --> 00:11:47.870
We can debut [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:49.347
So this what we want.

00:11:49.347 --> 00:11:50.930
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's what you need?

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:51.310
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: Yes.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.851
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's good to know.

00:11:52.851 --> 00:11:54.350
We're doing this at Next.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.330
Outside of seeing
the on-prem for GKE,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.310
what else have you seen that
you've been excited about?

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:03.024
TERRY LI: I'm excited
about the auto ML stuff.

00:12:03.024 --> 00:12:04.940
I mean, I'm not an expert
on machine learning,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:06.670
but I used to play with it.

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.950
And I spent a lot of time
trying to build a super-model.

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:12.680
And seeing that being
automated and seeing

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:16.280
I can train a model in
a few clicks, I think,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.830
will save a lot of effort
for many machine learning

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:20.120
engineers.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.580
And I think it'll
also allow people

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:26.900
from business intelligence team
to be able to analyze our log

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.930
and automate if they find
interesting conclusions

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:34.230
of our user behaviors, which is
exciting to me and to the team,

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:34.790
I guess.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:36.950
MELANIE WARRICK: So do you
have certain ways right now

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.075
that come to mind on how
you'd want to implement it

00:12:39.075 --> 00:12:40.327
once it's made available?

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:46.530
TERRY LI: Because our logs
are already in BigQuery,

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:50.120
and I saw a demo of building
models based on BigQuery data,

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:54.680
I can imagine people without
machine learning skill easily

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:59.510
do user classification or
analyze some user behavior

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:01.796
based on the auto ML technology.

00:13:01.796 --> 00:13:03.920
MELANIE WARRICK: Based on
your specific experiences

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.420
with your user base
and the content

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.200
that you have on your users?

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.320
Yeah, that would be
wonderful, especially

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:10.820
in terms of being
able to specialize

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:12.110
your interactions with them.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.604
I know that's a leading need
for many customers out there.

00:13:14.604 --> 00:13:15.770
MARK MANDEL: What about you?

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:18.228
What have you happened to see
that you've been particularly

00:13:18.228 --> 00:13:19.040
excited about?

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.250
TAICHI NAKASHIMA: I'm excited
about the cloud service

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:23.660
platform, CSP.

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:25.400
I don't know what
exactly that is,

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:29.870
but I saw the line about
mange the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.450
For microservice platform,
I think [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.530
would be the key technology.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.140
So I want to use [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:37.370
So I'm very excited.

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:38.870
MELANIE WARRICK:
[INAUDIBLE] is very

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.120
valuable for being able to
track, especially with so

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.550
many systems distributed.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.197
Yeah, for tracking how
performance is happening.

00:13:45.197 --> 00:13:47.780
MARK MANDEL: So I'd love to go
back to and hear more about how

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:49.280
your migration is going.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:53.000
Because it sounds like you've
gone from on-prem monolith

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.960
to cloud microservices
in one go?

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:57.890
Is that accurate?

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:00.410
How has that journey been?

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:03.560
TERRY LI: We started
doing it, and we have

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.540
deployed some services own GKE.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:10.140
But the majority of the back-end
is still in our data center

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:14.040
and will probably take a
much longer time to do that.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:16.589
MARK MANDEL: How have you
been tearing it apart?

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:17.630
What are you doing there?

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:18.470
How's that working?

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:20.780
TERRY LI: We're trying to
find the most important

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:23.500
or the most fundamental
services, like user services

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:26.510
and item services.

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:29.450
An item is something
that a user try to sell.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:31.880
Finding these most
fundamental services

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.354
and make them as microservices,
so that some other team,

00:14:35.354 --> 00:14:36.770
when they need a
new feature, they

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:39.690
can utilize these services
to build new features.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.480
So we start from
there, and we try

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:46.220
to move as much as the
system to GKE as possible.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.320
MARK MANDEL: And why
are you doing this?

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.240
TERRY LI: I think we
are at a point where

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:54.590
we have more than 100 engineers
working on the same repository.

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.060
And it's, like,
half million lines

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:58.670
of code to make a
simple code change.

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:00.470
To modify some
part of a feature,

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:02.060
you have to deploy
the whole thing

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:04.040
and, fingers crossed,
nothing breaks.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.840
MELANIE WARRICK:
Because it never does.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:06.881
TERRY LI: Right, exactly.

00:15:06.881 --> 00:15:09.880
So I think that's
the main reason--

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.916
to make our organization
more scalable.

00:15:11.916 --> 00:15:13.040
MARK MANDEL: Why the cloud?

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:15.170
Because you could do micro
services on your own hardware.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.330
Why are you coming to the cloud?

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:17.955
TAICHI NAKASHIMA:
The reason the cloud.

00:15:17.955 --> 00:15:19.660
So as I said before,
each developer

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:22.250
need to operate their service.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:24.800
They also need to operate
the database itself.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.130
So we want manage
everything in on premise

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:30.590
is super hard for
normal developers.

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:33.460
If we use the cloud,
they have use of API.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:36.170
And the developer easily
set up the database

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:37.720
of these managed services.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:40.201
So that's why we need
move to the cloud.

00:15:40.201 --> 00:15:40.950
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:45.155
How are you controlling what
developers are able to do what?

00:15:45.155 --> 00:15:47.780
Are you just like, they
can do anything, it's fine?

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:49.924
Or you're being a
bit more restrictive?

00:15:49.924 --> 00:15:51.340
TAICHI NAKASHIMA:
At [INAUDIBLE],,

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:54.800
we use the Terraform to
set up the cloud services.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.825
So currently, we manage
whole Terraform configuration

00:15:57.825 --> 00:15:58.982
in one repository.

00:15:58.982 --> 00:16:02.410
We prepare directory
for each microservices.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.350
And each microservice team
try to send a pull request

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:06.560
to these directory.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:10.710
And the microservice [INAUDIBLE]
team is always debut that call.

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:15.450
And we check the developer
does not do the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:16.665
So we try to limit that way.

00:16:16.665 --> 00:16:18.790
MELANIE WARRICK: You've
already talked a little bit

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.550
about this in terms of the
value of having microservices.

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.740
But if you were to do
this all over again,

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:27.010
tips, ideas, lessons learned
that you'd recommend?

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.657
TERRY LI: Start from the cloud.

00:16:28.657 --> 00:16:29.740
Don't make any monolithic.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:33.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.770
I mean, we started
five years ago.

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:37.900
My colleagues started
five years ago.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.460
They probably didn't
have the option

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:42.250
to start from what we have here.

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:43.846
That's why we're doing this.

00:16:43.846 --> 00:16:45.220
MELANIE WARRICK:
Which is common.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:45.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, but
there are definitely

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:47.960
lots of people who are
in similar situations.

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:50.960
So if you were like, this is
the best way we've worked out

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:53.860
to be, this is how we do the
conversion of microservices,

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.390
or don't do it this way,
what would you recommend?

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.460
TERRY LI: I feel like we are
trying to make too many changes

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:01.990
at the same time.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.660
We were trying to
rewrite the code from PHP

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:08.380
with Golang We are trying
to deploy our services

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:11.800
from on-prem DC to the cloud.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.740
And we're trying to migrate
the database from there

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:16.240
to the cloud as well.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.609
The design is from
a monolithic web app

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.010
to microservice architecture.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:24.589
So we're trying to
make so many changes.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:27.460
I think the difficulty
explodes and sometimes

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.435
make things more difficult
than it should be.

00:17:29.435 --> 00:17:31.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Before we
let you both go, anything else

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:33.880
that you wanted to
share, any places

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:36.880
that people can find more
information about Mercari

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:38.400
outside of the website?

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.690
TERRY LI: We have
an engineering blog.

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:41.830
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:43.677
TERRY LI: It's mostly
