00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.255
MELANIE WARRICK:
All right, Mark,

00:18:57.255 --> 00:18:59.440
how do I use my existing
identity management system

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:00.790
with Google Cloud Platform?

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:01.270
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:03.395
This is actually a really
interesting question too.

00:19:03.395 --> 00:19:05.470
I didn't even realize
this was a thing really.

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:07.960
So basically, by
default, Google Cloud

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:09.280
is tied to Google accounts.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:10.930
That's kind of how it was built.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.750
But you may have your own
identity management system.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:15.910
Maybe you use LDAP or
probably some other things

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:17.120
that I'm not even aware of.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.510
And you may want to use
that instead, or basically,

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:22.390
integrate it with Google
Cloud, so that you don't have

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:23.890
to reinvent the
wheel, essentially,

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:25.220
which I think is really cool.

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:27.100
So there's an article
that we will link to.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.470
We'll give you the top
level highlights here that I

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:30.880
think are most important.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.161
But the great thing
is you can do this.

00:19:33.161 --> 00:19:35.410
So if you have an existing
identity management system,

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:36.784
you can integrate this thing.

00:19:36.784 --> 00:19:38.950
There are a set of prerequisites
to getting started.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:41.320
You need to identify a domain
that you'll use to set up

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:42.675
your Cloud Identity account.

00:19:42.675 --> 00:19:44.050
You'll need to
make sure that you

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:46.930
have someone who can make text
or CNAME changes to your DNS

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:47.710
settings.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:50.232
And also, to allow your
users to authenticate

00:19:50.232 --> 00:19:51.940
against your identity
[INAUDIBLE] system,

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:54.805
you'll need to implement
SAML single sign on.

00:19:54.805 --> 00:19:56.430
We'll talk a little
bit about that too.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:57.960
So if you can do
those three things,

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.880
you can configure the ability
to use third-party identity

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:01.600
management.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:02.200
MELANIE WARRICK:
Couple other things

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:03.820
we want to mention
from the article

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:06.790
as well is that they recommend
doing a proof of concept

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:10.870
to basically don't synchronize
everything all at once.

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:14.500
Try synchronizing a small number
of users or a small group.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.510
And experiment with
that first to make sure

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:19.150
that your criteria
and your configuration

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:22.150
is properly set before
you do synchronization.

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:24.700
They also mention you don't
need to necessarily map

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.207
your entire organization.

00:20:26.207 --> 00:20:27.790
It's important to
plan what you really

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:30.160
need to synchronize
and then go from there.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:33.070
They do have some pointers
around mapping out

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:34.720
the functional
roles for your users

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:37.740
and creating groups
to map to those roles.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.660
Grant roles to a
Google Group instead of

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.484
to individual users
when possible,

00:20:41.484 --> 00:20:43.900
because it's easier to add and
remove members from a group

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:47.330
than it is to update
the IAM policy itself.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.000
So those are some tips.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:53.230
And they also do not
default transfer passwords.

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:55.410
They will do a
transfer of the users.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:57.940
But the passwords themselves
are not necessarily

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:59.464
synced up on default.

00:20:59.464 --> 00:21:00.880
MARK MANDEL: And
it's worth noting

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:02.680
that there are two
different ways of doing

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:03.513
the synchronization.

00:21:03.513 --> 00:21:05.110
There's Google Cloud
directories sync,

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:07.090
which will sync on a schedule.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:09.610
But then you can also have
third-party identity providers

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.260
do a direct connection as well.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.720
Each kind of has it's
pros and cons depending on

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:15.250
what systems you
already have set,

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:16.810
how easy it is to
use third party,

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:18.643
or whether you just
want to do regular syncs

00:21:18.643 --> 00:21:19.455
on a regular basis.

00:21:19.455 --> 00:21:21.190
But you can look at
both options as well.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:22.981
MELANIE WARRICK: And
the main thing to note

00:21:22.981 --> 00:21:25.810
is that I know it's
difficult to spend time

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.570
when you've got so many other
things going on to think

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:29.740
about identity management.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:31.570
But it's really,
crucial, and it's useful,

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:32.500
and it's important.

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:35.290
And resources like this are
helpful in trying to make

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:36.695
it a little easier for you.

